  "appreciation": "此诗为西周末年政治讽刺诗。开篇以日食、地震等罕见自然异象起笔，渲染出天崩地裂的末世氛围，暗喻周室将亡。随后笔锋直指朝政，痛斥皇父等佞臣乱政、褒姒干政，揭露其强拆民屋、聚敛财富、迁居避祸的恶行。诗人将天灾与人祸紧密结合，借自然异象批判政治黑暗，最后以“下民之孽，匪降自天”点明主旨，强调灾祸根源在于人事。全诗叙事与抒情交融，现实批判深刻，情感沉痛愤激，堪称先秦政讽诗杰作。",
  "english": "At the turn of the tenth month, / The new moon fell on Xinmao day. / The sun was eclipsed, / A most ominous sign. / Lately the moon was dimmed, / Now the sun is dimmed; / For the people below, / Sorrow is profound. / Sun and moon announce disaster, / Not following their proper paths. / The four quarters lack good governance, / Because good men are not employed. / When the moon is eclipsed, / It is but the common way; / But when the sun is eclipsed, / What ill fortune is this! / Lightning flashes and thunder rolls, / No peace, no blessing. / A hundred rivers boil, / Mountain peaks crumble. / High banks become valleys, / Deep valleys rise as hills. / Alas for the people of today, / Why are they not warned by such calamity? / Huangfu is the Minister, / Fan is the Minister of Instruction, / Jiabo is the Steward, / Zhongyun is the Chef, / Zouzi is the Archivist, / Jue is the Master of Horse, / Yu is the Tutor. / The favored wife wields power in her place. / Alas, this Huangfu, / Can he say the time is not right? / Why does he conscript me, / Without consulting me beforehand? / He strips my walls and roofs, / My fields are all flooded and overgrown. / He says, I do not harm you, / It is but the rule of rites. / Huangfu, so sage, / Builds a capital at Xiang. / He chooses three ministers, / Truly hoarding much wealth. / He spares not one elder, / To guard our king. / He selects those with horses and chariots, / And moves them to Xiang. / I toil diligently, / Not daring to complain of weariness. / Without crime or guilt, / Slanderous mouths clamor. / The people's afflictions, / Do not fall from heaven. / Gossiping and backbiting, / All strife is caused by men. / Endless is my sorrow, / A heavy malady indeed. / The four quarters have surplus, / I alone dwell in grief. / All men seek ease, / I alone dare not rest. / Heaven's mandate is not constant, / I dare not imitate my friends in seeking ease.",
  "source": "chinese-poetry",
  "enriched": true,
