  "appreciation": "此诗为《诗经·大雅》名篇，赞颂周宣王重臣仲山甫之德才。全诗以“天生烝民”起兴，将天命与道德相系，奠定庄严基调。接着以细腻笔触描绘仲山甫柔嘉维则、谨慎从政的品格，又以“柔亦不茹，刚亦不吐”的对比，凸显其刚正不阿、不畏强权之骨气。诗中“德輶如毛”的哲思与“衮职有阙”的隐喻，既赞其补衮之忠，又含劝勉之意。末章写其赴齐筑城，以“穆如清风”收束，情真意切，意境高远，堪称颂赞诗之典范。",
  "english": "Heaven gave birth to the multitudes, / All things have their laws. / The people hold to their nature, / Loving this virtuous beauty. / Heaven watches over the Zhou, / Its light descends below. / To protect the Son of Heaven, / It gave birth to Zhongshan Fu. / The virtue of Zhongshan Fu, / Gentle and good is his rule. / Fine bearing and fine countenance, / Cautious and reverent. / He models on ancient teachings, / Strives to maintain dignity. / He complies with the Son of Heaven, / And proclaims his clear commands. / The King commands Zhongshan Fu, / To be a model for all lords, / To continue his ancestors' work, / And protect the King's person. / Issuing and receiving the King's commands, / He is the King's mouthpiece. / Proclaiming policies abroad, / The four quarters thus respond. / Solemn is the King's command, / Zhongshan Fu executes it. / Whether states are good or bad, / Zhongshan Fu discerns it. / Both wise and discerning, / He preserves his person. / Morning and night without slack, / He serves the one man. / People also have a saying: / The soft they eat, / The hard they spit out. / But Zhongshan Fu, / The soft he eats not, / The hard he spits not. / He wrongs not the widows, / He fears not the strong. / People also have a saying: / Virtue is light as hair, / But few can lift it. / I ponder and consider, / Only Zhongshan Fu lifts it. / We love him but cannot help him. / If the royal robe has a flaw, / Only Zhongshan Fu mends it. / Zhongshan Fu sets out to sacrifice. / Four stallions stand tall. / The travelers are swift, / Each fears to lag behind. / Four steeds gallop, / Eight bells ring clear. / The King commands Zhongshan Fu, / To build walls in the East. / Four steeds strong, / Eight bells chime. / Zhongshan Fu goes to Qi, / May he soon return. / Jifu composed this ode, / Gentle as the clear breeze. / Zhongshan Fu holds long thoughts, / To comfort his heart.",
  "source": "chinese-poetry",
