  "appreciation": "此诗为周宣王平定淮夷、册命召虎的纪功之作。全诗以江汉浩荡起兴，极写武夫威武雄壮，烘托出王者之师的磅礴气势。后文转入宣王对召虎的册命与赏赐，辞气温雅庄重。诗篇不仅歌颂赫赫武功，更强调“矢其文德，洽此四国”，将武功与文治交融。叙事点面结合，既有宏大的战争与开国气象，又有具体的君臣对答细节，辞严义密，气象宏大，是西周中兴时代的壮丽颂歌。",
  "english": "The Yangtze and Han rivers surge and swell, / The warriors march with billowing might. / Not seeking ease, not wandering free, / To chastise the Huai tribes they go. / Our war-chariots are drawn out, / Our banners are set up high. / Not seeking ease, not lingering slow, / To crush the Huai tribes they go. / The Yangtze and Han rivers broadly roll, / The warriors stand fierce and grand. / They govern and pacify all sides, / And report success to the King. / The four quarters are now at peace, / The royal domain is settled. / Now there are no more wars or strife, / The King's heart is at rest. / By the banks of the Yangtze and Han, / The King commands Shao Hu: / Open up the four quarters, / Survey and mark our lands. / Do not be harsh, do not be rash, / Let the royal realm be the standard. / Marking boundaries and governing lands, / All the way to the southern sea. / The King commands Shao Hu: / Go survey and proclaim the word. / When Wen and Wu received the mandate, / Duke Shao was their strong pillar. / Do not say I am but a youth, / You are the likeness of Duke Shao. / Swiftly and diligently do your duty, / And Heaven will bestow its blessing. / I grant you a jade scepter and cup, / And a flask of fragrant black millet wine. / Proclaim it to your ancestors, / I gift you mountains and fields. / You receive the mandate from Zhou, / Just as your ancestor Shao did. / Hu bows his head to the earth: / Long live the Son of Heaven! / Hu bows his head to the earth, / To praise and answer the King's grace. / He makes a hymn for Duke Shao: / May the Son of Heaven live long! / Bright and shining is our King, / His good name shall never end. / He displays his civil virtue, / To harmonize these four lands.",
  "source": "chinese-poetry",
  "enriched": true,
  "englishBy": "glm-5.2 译",
