  "appreciation": "此章借古喻今，以商汤与周武王行王政之史实，阐明“仁者无敌”之理。行文跌宕起伏，先述汤助葛而葛伯仇饷，反衬汤之仁至义尽；后写汤武征伐时百姓“若大旱之望雨”的渴盼，生动刻画出王政如“时雨降”般的救民水火之效。东西南北之怨与“归市弗止”的对比，凸显民心向背。末句“齐楚虽大，何畏焉”掷地有声，以气势磅礴的反问收束全篇，将王道之威严与霸道之虚弱判然两分，极具雄辩力量与感染力。",
  "english": "Mencius said: 'Tang lived in Bo, neighboring the state of Ge. The Earl of Ge was reckless and did not offer sacrifices. / Tang sent someone to ask him: \"Why do you not sacrifice?\" He replied: \"I have no animals for the offerings.\" Tang sent him sheep and oxen. The Earl of Ge ate them and still did not sacrifice. / Tang again sent someone to ask: \"Why do you not sacrifice?\" He replied: \"I have no grain for the offerings.\" Tang sent the people of Bo to plow for them, with the old and weak delivering food. / The Earl of Ge led his people to intercept and rob those who had wine, food, and grain, killing those who refused. A boy bringing grain and meat was killed and robbed. / The Book of Documents says: \"The Earl of Ge treated the bringers of food as enemies.\" This refers to that. Because he killed this boy, Tang punished him, and all within the Four Seas said: \"He does not covet the wealth of the world, but seeks vengeance for ordinary men and women.\" / \"Tang's first punishment began with Ge\"; after eleven campaigns, he was invincible. When he campaigned to the east, the western Yi complained; when he campaigned to the south, the northern Di complained, saying: \"Why are we left for last?\" / The people longed for him as they long for rain in a great drought. Those in the markets did not stop, the farmers did not change their work. He executed their rulers and comforted the people, like a timely rain falling. The people were greatly pleased. / The Book of Documents says: \"We wait for our lord; when he comes, we shall suffer no more.\" / \"Those who did not submit were campaigned against by the King of Zhou moving east, bringing peace to the men and women. They filled baskets with black and yellow silks to seek an audience with the King of Zhou and submitted to the great capital of Zhou.\" / The officers welcomed the Zhou officers with baskets of silks, and the common people welcomed the Zhou soldiers with baskets of food and pots of drink, saving the people from flood and fire, and merely removing the tyrant. / The Great Declaration says: \"Our martial prowess is displayed, invading their borders, removing the tyrant, expanding the punishment of slaughter, outshining even Tang.\" / It is merely that one does not practice the Royal Way; if one practices it, all within the Four Seas will raise their heads and look up to him, wishing him to be their ruler. Though Qi and Chu are large, what is there to fear?'",
  "source": "chinese-poetry",
